        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="2">
                    <English>Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The following areas are those which are showing signs of air leakage:



Around exterior doors - could replace weatherstripping and door sweeps to prevent air from entering. Easy fix instead of replacing the whole door.



The was a dryer vent to the exterior that was letting air in - If not in use than this should be sealed



Furnace air intake through the foundation wall - Caulking or spray foam around the edges



Plumbing rough-in through foundation - Caulking or spray foam around the edges



Air is entering through attic hatches - Properly seal around attic hatches with a rubber seal or gasket



There a 2 old Metal flaps in the foundation wall behind the furnace that are letting air into the house - These should be properly sealed over to avoid air leakage.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Add 2 " of rigid board to the exterior of the entire house</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>I noticed some vermicultie insulation under the cellulose and batt insultion in the ceiling of the original house. This is only a very small amount of insulation and is not doing any harm as it is not being disturbed. Some types of vermiculite can contain asbestos. If this is a concern you may want to contact a certified professional.</ceilings>
                <foundation>May want to insulate the foundation in the future. This can be done from either outside or inside. </foundation>
                <windows>Windows in the basement should be upgraded to Energy Star rated windows for zone 3</windows>
                <doors>Weatherstripping around exterior doors</doors>
                <HeatingSystem>The furnace is going to need to be upgraded at some point in the future. If you decide to upgrade your heating system than there are a few options to consider.



If you have a 200A main electrical panel than you could  have an electric furnace installed.



A condensing propane furnace is also a good option for an upgrade. These units are typically 95% efficient. You would not need a chimney as the exhaust can be vented with a pvc pipe through the wall. 



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>Havea great east/west roof for a solar photovoltaic system. There are many benefits of installing a solar system as well as great incentives from government organizations as well.</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>Water pipes within 3-4 meters of the water tank should be insulated. </WaterConservation>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
